## How HashMap Works Internally

Understanding the internal mechanics makes every interview question about maps intuitive. HashMap is fundamentally an array of linked lists (or trees) indexed by hash code.

### The Put/Get Algorithm

When you call `map.put("Alice", 100)`, HashMap does this:

1. Compute `"Alice".hashCode()` → e.g., `63476538`
2. Reduce to an array index: `hash & (table.length - 1)` → e.g., `6` (for a 16-bucket table)
3. Walk the linked list (or tree) at `table[6]` looking for a key that `.equals("Alice")`
4. If found, replace the value. If not found, add a new node at the head of the chain.

```java
// Simplified put logic
int index = hash(key) & (capacity - 1);   // which bucket?
Node<K,V> node = table[index];
while (node != null) {
    if (node.hash == hash && node.key.equals(key)) {
        node.value = value;  // replace existing
        return;
    }
    node = node.next;
}
table[index] = new Node<>(hash, key, value, table[index]); // prepend
size++;
```

This is why both `hashCode()` and `equals()` matter: `hashCode()` picks the bucket, `equals()` identifies the exact key within that bucket.

### Collision Handling: List → Tree (Java 8+)

When many keys hash to the same bucket, the linked list grows and lookup degrades to O(n). Java 8 introduced **treeification**: when a bucket's chain reaches 8 entries, it converts to a balanced red-black tree, bringing worst-case lookup from O(n) to O(log n). When the tree shrinks below 6 entries, it converts back to a linked list.

This was a security fix — attackers could craft keys with identical hash codes (hash flooding), degrading HashMap to O(n²) for lookups. Treeification caps the damage at O(n log n).

### Load Factor and Resizing

HashMap starts with 16 buckets and a load factor of 0.75. When `size > capacity * loadFactor` (12 entries for default), it **resizes**: allocates a new array of double the size and rehashes every entry. Resizing is O(n) — every key must be recomputed to find its new bucket.

```java
// Pre-sizing to avoid resizing — pass expected size / load factor
int expectedEntries = 1000;
Map<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>(
    (int) (expectedEntries / 0.75) + 1  // 1334 initial capacity
);
```

### Why HashTable Is Dead

HashTable synchronizes every method with `synchronized` — even read-only operations like `get()` block all other threads. In a system with 90% reads and 10% writes, readers needlessly block each other.

ConcurrentHashMap (Java 8+) uses a fundamentally better approach:
- **Reads are lock-free**: `get()` uses volatile reads — no lock at all
- **Writes lock only the affected bucket**: other buckets remain accessible
- **CAS operations** (Compare-And-Swap) for atomic counter updates and node insertion

```java
// ConcurrentHashMap — atomic compute without external locking
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> counts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
counts.merge("page_views", 1, Integer::sum);  // atomic increment
// No synchronized block, no race condition, no full-map lock
```

The performance difference is dramatic: under 16 threads, ConcurrentHashMap is 10-50x faster than Hashtable or `Collections.synchronizedMap()`.

### Null Key Handling

HashMap allows one null key (stored at bucket 0) and any number of null values. ConcurrentHashMap and Hashtable prohibit null keys and values — throwing `NullPointerException`.

Why? In a concurrent map, `map.get(key)` returning `null` is ambiguous: does the key not exist, or does it exist with a null value? HashMap can resolve this with `containsKey()`, but in a concurrent map the state can change between the two calls. Banning nulls removes the ambiguity entirely.

// HashMap — your default choice
Map<String, Integer> scores = new HashMap<>();
scores.put(null, 100);        // OK — one null key allowed
scores.put("Alice", null);    // OK — null values allowed
scores.put("Bob", 95);

// HashTable — legacy, don't use
Map<String, Integer> legacy = new Hashtable<>();
// legacy.put(null, 100);     // NullPointerException!
// legacy.put("Alice", null); // NullPointerException!

// ConcurrentHashMap — for thread-safe needs (modern)
Map<String, Integer> concurrent = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
concurrent.put("Alice", 100); // thread-safe, no full-map lock
// concurrent.put(null, 100); // NullPointerException! (no null keys)
// concurrent.put("Bob", null); // NullPointerException! (no null values)

// HOW HASHMAP HANDLES COLLISIONS (Java 8+)
// When two keys hash to the same bucket:
// - First 8 entries: linked list (O(n) worst case)
// - After 8 entries: converts to balanced red-black tree (O(log n))
// This prevents pathological O(n) performance from hash collisions

// HashMap internal structure (simplified)
// table[0] → null
// table[1] → Entry("Alice", 100) → null
// table[2] → Entry("Bob", 95) → Entry("Charlie", 88) → null  // collision chain
// table[3] → null

// PRACTICAL: Thread-safe patterns

// Pattern 1: ConcurrentHashMap (best for concurrent read/write)
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
map.put("counter", 0);
map.compute("counter", (k, v) -> v + 1);  // atomic read-modify-write

// Pattern 2: computeIfAbsent for lazy initialization
Map<String, List<String>> groupedData = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
groupedData.computeIfAbsent("fruits", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add("apple");

// Pattern 3: Collections.synchronizedMap (simpler but slower)
Map<String, Integer> syncMap = Collections.synchronizedMap(new HashMap<>());
// Must manually synchronize iteration:
synchronized (syncMap) {
    for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : syncMap.entrySet()) {
        System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue());
    }
}

// List — ordered, allows duplicates
List<String> shoppingCart = new ArrayList<>();
shoppingCart.add("Milk");
shoppingCart.add("Bread");
shoppingCart.add("Milk");   // allowed! duplicate
System.out.println(shoppingCart);        // [Milk, Bread, Milk]
System.out.println(shoppingCart.get(0)); // Milk — index access

// Set — unique elements, no duplicates
Set<String> visitedPages = new HashSet<>();
visitedPages.add("/home");
visitedPages.add("/about");
visitedPages.add("/home");  // ignored! already exists
System.out.println(visitedPages);          // [/home, /about] (order not guaranteed)
System.out.println(visitedPages.contains("/home")); // true — O(1) lookup

// LinkedHashSet — unique + preserves insertion order
Set<String> orderedUnique = new LinkedHashSet<>();
orderedUnique.add("first");
orderedUnique.add("second");
orderedUnique.add("first"); // ignored
System.out.println(orderedUnique); // [first, second] — insertion order kept

// DEDUPLICATION PATTERNS

// Remove duplicates from a list while preserving order
List<String> withDupes = Arrays.asList("a", "b", "a", "c", "b");
List<String> unique = new ArrayList<>(new LinkedHashSet<>(withDupes));
// Result: [a, b, c] — order preserved, duplicates removed

// Stream API alternative (Java 8+)
List<String> unique2 = withDupes.stream()
    .distinct()  // uses equals() and hashCode()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());

// TREESET — sorted unique elements
Set<Integer> sorted = new TreeSet<>();
sorted.add(30);
sorted.add(10);
sorted.add(20);
System.out.println(sorted);  // [10, 20, 30] — automatically sorted

// TreeSet with custom Comparator
Set<String> caseInsensitive = new TreeSet<>(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER);
caseInsensitive.add("Alice");
caseInsensitive.add("alice");  // ignored — same as "Alice" case-insensitively
System.out.println(caseInsensitive);  // [Alice]

// IMMUTABLE COLLECTIONS (Java 9+)
List<String> immutableList = List.of("a", "b", "c");
// immutableList.add("d");  // UnsupportedOperationException

Set<String> immutableSet = Set.of("a", "b", "c");
// immutableSet.add("d");   // UnsupportedOperationException

// ENUMSET — specialized Set for enum types, extremely fast
enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE }
Set<Color> colors = EnumSet.of(Color.RED, Color.BLUE);  // bit-vector internally
